<item>
  <title>Flows Explained Simply: A Visual Guide</title>
  <dc:creator>Hujie Wang</dc:creator>
  <link>https://layernorm.dev/posts/diffusion/2-flows/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<section id="flow" class="level1">
<h1>Flow</h1>
<section id="intuition-think-of-a-river" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="intuition-think-of-a-river">Intuition: Think of a River</h2>
<p>Imagine a river with currents flowing in different directions at different locations.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vector field</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?u_t(x)">: The current at each point — it tells a particle “if you’re here, move in this direction”</li>
<li><strong>Trajectory</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X_t">: Following one leaf dropped in the river — where is <em>that specific leaf</em> at time <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t">?</li>
<li><strong>Flow</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi_t(x)">: A map that answers “where does <em>any</em> leaf end up at time <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t">, given it started at position <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x">?”</li>
</ul>
<p>The key insight: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X_t"> tracks one particle, while <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi_t"> describes how the <em>entire space</em> transforms over time.</p>
<div id="fig-single-trajectory" class="quarto-float quarto-figure quarto-figure-center anchored">
<figure class="quarto-float quarto-float-fig figure">
<div aria-describedby="fig-single-trajectory-caption-0ceaefa1-69ba-4598-a22c-09a6ac19f8ca">
<img src="https://layernorm.dev/posts/diffusion/2-flows/single-trajectory.png" class="img-fluid figure-img">
</div>
<figcaption class="quarto-float-caption-bottom quarto-float-caption quarto-float-fig" id="fig-single-trajectory-caption-0ceaefa1-69ba-4598-a22c-09a6ac19f8ca">
Figure&nbsp;1: A single trajectory following the vector field
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>
<section id="formal-definition" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="formal-definition">Formal Definition</h2>
<p>Every ODE is defined by a vector field <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?u_t(x)">. A solution <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X_t"> starting from <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x_0"> must satisfy:</p>
<p><span id="eq-vector-field"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cfrac%7BdX_t%7D%7Bdt%7D%20&amp;=%20u_t(X_t)%20%5C%5C%0AX_0%20&amp;=%20x_0%20%5Cquad%20(%5Ctext%7Binitial%20condition%7D)%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B1%7D"></span></p>
<p>In words: “your velocity equals whatever the vector field says at your current location.”</p>
</section>
<section id="from-trajectory-to-flow" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="from-trajectory-to-flow">From Trajectory to Flow</h2>
<p>The trajectory <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X_t"> answers: “where am I at time <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t">, given I started at <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x_0">?”</p>
<p>But what if we want to know the answer for <em>any</em> starting point? That’s the <strong>flow</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi_t(x)">: a function that takes any starting position <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x"> and returns where it ends up at time <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t">.</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AX_t%20=%20%5Cphi_t(x_0)%0A"></p>
<p>For one specific starting point <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x_0">, the trajectory and flow give the same answer. But the flow is more general — it’s the entire “transformation rule” for all points.</p>
<div id="fig-multiple-trajectories" class="quarto-float quarto-figure quarto-figure-center anchored">
<figure class="quarto-float quarto-float-fig figure">
<div aria-describedby="fig-multiple-trajectories-caption-0ceaefa1-69ba-4598-a22c-09a6ac19f8ca">
<img src="https://layernorm.dev/posts/diffusion/2-flows/multiple-trajectories.png" class="img-fluid figure-img">
</div>
<figcaption class="quarto-float-caption-bottom quarto-float-caption quarto-float-fig" id="fig-multiple-trajectories-caption-0ceaefa1-69ba-4598-a22c-09a6ac19f8ca">
Figure&nbsp;2: Multiple trajectories: the flow describes where ANY starting point ends up
</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>
<section id="flow-equation" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="flow-equation">Flow Equation</h2>
<p>Since the flow satisfies the ODE, we can substitute <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X_t%20=%20%5Cphi_t(x_0)">:</p>
<p><span id="eq-flow-ode"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Cfrac%7BdX_t%7D%7Bdt%7D%20&amp;=%20u_t(X_t)%20%5Cimplies%20%5Cfrac%7Bd%20%5Cphi_t(x_0)%7D%7Bdt%7D%20=%20u_t(%5Cphi_t(x_0))%20%5C%5C%0AX_0%20&amp;=%20x_0%20%5Cimplies%20%5Cphi_0(x_0)%20=%20x_0%0A%5Cend%7Baligned%7D%0A%5Ctag%7B2%7D"></span></p>
<p>The second line says: at <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t=0">, every point is where it started (the flow is the identity map).</p>
</section>
<section id="summary" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="summary">Summary</h2>
<table class="caption-top table">
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 25%">
<col style="width: 27%">
<col style="width: 47%">
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Concept</th>
<th>Notation</th>
<th>What it answers</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Vector field</td>
<td><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?u_t(x)"></td>
<td>“What direction should I move at position <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x">?”</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Trajectory</td>
<td><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X_t"></td>
<td>“Where is this one particle at time <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t">?”</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Flow</td>
<td><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cphi_t(x)"></td>
<td>“Where does any point <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x"> end up at time <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t">?”</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Vector fields define ODEs whose solutions are flows.</strong></p>
<p>Think of it like Google Maps:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vector field</strong> = Traffic directions at every location (“turn left here”, “go straight there”)</li>
<li><strong>ODE</strong> = The instruction “just follow the GPS”</li>
<li><strong>Trajectory</strong> = The route YOUR car takes</li>
<li><strong>Flow</strong> = If 1000 cars started at different places and all followed GPS, the flow tells you where ALL of them end up</li>
</ul>
<p>So: arrows (vector field) + “follow the arrows” (ODE) = everyone’s destination (flow).</p>


</section>
</section>

 ]]></description>
